The	  nature	  of	  the	  anions	  and	  the	  M:L	  ratio	  are	  also	  key parameters	   for	   the	   formation	   of	   well-­‐defined	   Cu-­‐based MOFs.	  The	  use	  of $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ and	  a	  2:1	  (M:L2)	  ratio	  resulted in	   ill-­‐defined	   structures,	   contrasted	   with	   the	   higher	   degree	   of	   uniformity	   obtained	   for	   a	   1:1	   ratio.	   The	   latter	   resulted	   in	   flower-­‐like	   morphologies $(\mathbf{Cu(NO_{3})L2}$ ;	   Figure	   7 and	   Figure $\mathrm{S}_{13}\mathrm{\dot{\Omega}}$ ).	   Interestingly,	   performing	   this	   reaction with	   rigorous	   exclusion	   of	   air	   and	   use	   of	   dry	   solvents resulted	   in	   the	   formation	   of	   rectangular	   prisms	   with	   an average	   length	   of $3.65\pm\:0.95\:\mu\mathrm{m}$ and	   breadth	   of ${\bf0.675\pm}$ 0.09 $\mu\mathrm{m}$ $({\bf C u}({\bf N O}_{3}){\bf L}\mathfrak{z}^{\#}$ ;	   Figure	   8).	   In	   contrast	   to	   the	   other Cu-­‐based	  MOFs, $\mathbf{\hat{C}u}(\mathbf{NO}_{3})\mathbf{L}\mathbf{2}^{\#}$ does	  not	  show	  any	  evidence of	  interpenetration.	  The	  presence	  and	  coordination	  of	  L2 is	   confirmed	   by	   FT-­‐IR	   spectroscopy	   showing	   peaks corresponding	   to	   the	   ligand	   framework	   of	   CuBrL2, $\mathbf{Cu}(\mathbf{NO}_{3})\mathbf{L}\mathbf{2}$ ,	   and $({\bf C u}({\bf N O}_{3}^{\top}){{\bf L}}\mathfrak{z}^{\#}$ (Figure	   S14).	   The	   crystal-­‐ line	   nature	   of	   these $\mathtt{C u}$ -­‐based	   MOFs	   was	   unequivocally demonstrated	  by $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -­‐ray	   diffraction.	   Powder	   X-­‐ray	   diffraction	   (PXRD)	   patterns	   are	   shown	   in	   Figure $\mathrm{\sfS3C-E}$ .	   The elemental	   composition	   of	   the	   MOFs	   was	   qualitatively confirmed	   by	   EDS	   (Figure $S_{15}\mathrm{\dot{}}$ ).	   TGA	   showed	   a	   relatively small	   weight	   decrease	   of $4{\cdot}5\mathrm{~\ensuremath~{~-~}~}7{\cdot}4\mathrm{~\ensuremath~{~\%~}~}$ at $6{\scriptstyle1-67^{\circ}C}$ corresponding	  with	  the	  loss	  of $\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ (Figure	  S8C-­‐E).
Dissolving	  the $\mathtt{C u}$ -­‐based	  MOFs	  under	  acidic	  conditions, and	   subsequent	   isolation	   and	   characterization	   of	   L2	   by NMR	   spectroscopy	   and	   mass-­‐spectrometry	   confirmed	   its stability	   (Figure	   S6B).	   The	   three	   Cu-­‐based	   MOFs	   were found	   to	   be	   less	   uniform	   than	   NiClL1	   and	   NiBrL2, however,	   they	   still	   have	   a	   common	   structural	   motif.	   The lesser	  degree	  of	  uniformity	  for	  the	  Cu-­‐based	  MOFs	  might be	  related	  to	  their	  higher	  structural	  complexity	  and	  larger diversity	  of	  possible	  structures.
The	   formation	   of	   the	   MOFs	   is	   probably	   a	   result	   of	   a complex	   cascade	   of	   assembly	   processes.40,41	   For	   both	   Ni and $\mathtt{C u}$ -­‐based	  MOFs,	  the	  solvent	  composition	  plays	  a	  crucial	   role	   as	   well	   for	   the	   generation	   of	   uniform	   structures (Figures	   S2,	   S16	   and $S_{17}\mathrm{\Omega}$ ).	   Varying	   the	   DMF/ $\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ ratios and/or	   addition	   of	   other	   solvents	   (PhCN,	   DMSO,	   water) leads	  to	  different	  assemblies.
Extending	   the	   EM	   studies	   of	   the	   formation	   of	   the	   Niand $\mathtt{C u}$ -­‐based	   MOFs	   revealed	   interesting	   mechanistic information.	  A	  time-­‐dependent	  analysis	  showed	  distinctly different	   pathways	   for	   the	   formation	   of	   the	   uniform structures	   obtained.	   Mixing	   the	   solutions	   of $\mathsf{N i C l}_{2}$ and $\ensuremath{\mathrm{NiBr}}_{2}$ salts	  with	  the	  corresponding	  ligand	  (L1	  or	  L2)	  result in	   an	   immediate	   precipitation.	   Apparently,	   the	   process starts	   with	   the	   coordination	   of	   the	   ligand	   to	   the	   metal center	   as	   the	   first	   nucleation	   step	   as	   common	   in crystallizations	   and	   colloid	   synthesis.	   SEM	   analysis	   of NiClL1	   aliquots	   taken	   immediately	   upon	   mixing	   showed the	   formation	   of	   a	   mixture	   of	   elongated	   (needles)	   and cubical	  structures ${\bf\langle<1}\mu{\bf m}$ ;	  Figure	  9A).	  Thermolysis	  of	  this mixture	  resulted	  in	  the	  formation	  of	  premature	  hexagonal structures,	   whose	   overall	   shape	   and	   size	   resembles	   the final	   product,	   but	   with	   coarse	   texture	   and	   edges	   (Figure 9B).	   Continuous	   heating	   for	   5	   days	   afforded	   the	   polished
